"Doran Hallick comes to the Institute at seven each morning, by the side door on Mercer Street."
"Hallick is fifty, lean, ink-stained and quiet; he notices talent before he notices clothes."
"Fenner reports anyone odd at the gate to Hallick, not to the police."
"Near the Spire, Nala feels a faint pull behind her breastbone: the augera stirring toward her."
"The Spire soldiers are bored conscripts from the north who stop only those who touch the doors."
"The ink-stained man at Hobb's hatch is Doran Hallick, the Institute's master."
"Hallick marks anyone loitering by his Institute, and asks Wenna Hobb about them before noon."
"Tessa Voyle is failing her Signifier set and would pour out her troubles to any kind listener."
"Tessa's elder brother was called up this winter; she has had no letter from him in a month."
"Hallick lost a Geldor chair twenty years ago over a paper arguing glyphs have a grammar."
"Hallick still believes glyphs have a grammar, and a new idea about glyphs would hook him at once."
"Hallick is not after a better post; he wants to be proven right, and says so to no one."
"Close to, Hallick feels Nala's flare as dense and eerily still, like nothing he has met."
"That flare makes Hallick stop and look, whatever her pitch; he hides his interest behind curtness."
"Hallick would not report a paperless stranger to police; he has hidden strays before."
